1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,696
You'll hear that exact sentence end a review, and it's almost never specific enough

2
00:00:04,696 --> 00:00:05,586
to build against.

3
00:00:05,586 --> 00:00:06,476
In the loop when?

4
00:00:06,476 --> 00:00:07,261
Reviewing what?

5
00:00:07,261 --> 00:00:08,832
Before the action or after it?

6
00:00:08,832 --> 00:00:10,822
Those aren't details to fill in later.

7
00:00:10,822 --> 00:00:12,288
They're the entire decision.

8
00:00:12,288 --> 00:00:16,686
Put the human in the wrong place and you either add friction to something that never

9
00:00:16,686 --> 00:00:20,770
needed it, or you add a reassuring sentence to the PRD that catches nothing in

10
00:00:20,770 --> 00:00:21,241
practice.

11
00:00:21,241 --> 00:00:23,179
"Human in the loop" isn't one design.

12
00:00:23,179 --> 00:00:27,158
It's five, and the wrong one either burns your reviewers or catches nothing.

13
00:00:27,158 --> 00:00:31,399
Both mistakes are common, and they come from the same shortcut: picking a pattern

14
00:00:31,399 --> 00:00:35,378
because it's the one your team has used before, rather than asking what this

15
00:00:35,378 --> 00:00:37,054
specific feature actually needs.

16
00:00:37,054 --> 00:00:41,033
A pattern that worked perfectly for a low-stakes summariser can be genuinely

17
00:00:41,033 --> 00:00:45,379
dangerous applied to something that spends money, and a pattern built for something

18
00:00:45,379 --> 00:00:49,411
irreversible can smother a harmless drafting tool in approvals nobody needed.

19
00:00:49,961 --> 00:00:54,567
Read this table as a spectrum, from a human checking everything to a human checking

20
00:00:54,567 --> 00:00:58,229
almost nothing, and notice the right column is doing all the work.

21
00:00:58,229 --> 00:01:02,724
Pre-approval belongs to the act archetype at its most dangerous: money moving, an

22
00:01:02,724 --> 00:01:05,610
account changing, nothing recoverable if it's wrong.

23
00:01:05,610 --> 00:01:10,216
Draft-then-review is the natural home for anything a user reads directly, the human

24
00:01:10,216 --> 00:01:14,433
isn't approving an internal step, they're the last check before publication.

25
00:01:14,433 --> 00:01:18,817
Sampled audit and monitor-only both trade certainty for speed, appropriate once

26
00:01:18,817 --> 00:01:21,925
being wrong occasionally is merely annoying, not costly.

27
00:01:21,925 --> 00:01:26,419
Escalate on uncertainty is the most efficient pattern on this table, and the most

28
00:01:26,419 --> 00:01:31,081
dangerous one to pick too early, because it's only as good as the guardrail deciding

29
00:01:31,081 --> 00:01:32,468
what counts as uncertain.

30
00:01:32,468 --> 00:01:36,575
Notice, too, that these patterns aren't a ladder you climb once and leave.

31
00:01:36,575 --> 00:01:41,125
A feature often starts on draft-then-review while everyone's still learning how it

32
00:01:41,125 --> 00:01:45,787
fails, and earns its way down to sampled audit or escalation only once the pass rate

33
00:01:45,787 --> 00:01:50,226
and the failure taxonomy from the last two lectures have actually proven it out.

34
00:01:50,226 --> 00:01:54,887
Starting anywhere looser than that is borrowing trust the feature hasn't earned yet.

35
00:01:55,437 --> 00:02:00,112
Remember auto-approving refunds over five hundred dollars, from the disqualification

36
00:02:00,112 --> 00:02:01,782
checklist back in Section one.

37
00:02:01,782 --> 00:02:06,123
Here's the same feature, and here's why the pattern you pick isn't a footnote.

38
00:02:06,123 --> 00:02:10,798
Escalate on uncertainty sounds efficient, and it fails exactly where this feature is

39
00:02:10,798 --> 00:02:15,472
most dangerous: a model can be confidently wrong, the whole premise of hallucination

40
00:02:15,472 --> 00:02:17,031
from lecture one-point-five.

41
00:02:17,031 --> 00:02:21,149
A confident, wrong approval sails straight past a guardrail built to catch

42
00:02:21,149 --> 00:02:23,820
uncertainty, because the model wasn't uncertain.

43
00:02:23,820 --> 00:02:26,659
It was sure, and it was sure about the wrong thing.

44
00:02:26,659 --> 00:02:29,608
Pre-approval above a threshold doesn't have that gap.

45
00:02:29,608 --> 00:02:32,280
It doesn't ask the model whether it's confident.

46
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:36,787
It asks whether the dollar amount crosses a line, a question with no ambiguity at

47
00:02:36,787 --> 00:02:37,010
all.

48
00:02:37,560 --> 00:02:42,130
Say the honest caveat, because this is the failure mode that makes a technically

49
00:02:42,130 --> 00:02:44,357
correct design fail in practice anyway.

50
00:02:44,357 --> 00:02:46,585
Reviewer fatigue is real and it's fast.

51
00:02:46,585 --> 00:02:51,383
Give a human genuinely hard decisions all day and judgment holds up reasonably well.

52
00:02:51,383 --> 00:02:56,067
Give them two hundred near-identical approvals, most of them plainly fine, and the

53
00:02:56,067 --> 00:02:59,780
ninety-eighth one gets the same half-second glance as the second.

54
00:02:59,780 --> 00:03:02,008
The loop still exists on the org chart.

55
00:03:02,008 --> 00:03:05,549
It stopped doing anything useful somewhere around case thirty.

56
00:03:05,549 --> 00:03:10,176
Design against this directly: keep the volume any one reviewer sees low enough to

57
00:03:10,176 --> 00:03:14,860
stay real, rotate reviewers, or narrow pre-approval to genuinely high-stakes cases

58
00:03:14,860 --> 00:03:17,373
and route the rest to sampled audit instead.

59
00:03:17,373 --> 00:03:21,943
A human check that's too broad to sustain is worse than an honest, narrower one.

60
00:03:21,943 --> 00:03:26,684
This is worth putting a number on in the PRD itself, the same way the threshold and

61
00:03:26,684 --> 00:03:29,483
the failure floor got a number two lectures back.

62
00:03:29,483 --> 00:03:34,167
A review queue with no stated volume limit will grow until it breaks, quietly, and

63
00:03:34,167 --> 00:03:38,851
nobody will notice until an audit finds approvals that took less time to make than

64
00:03:38,851 --> 00:03:39,308
to read.

65
00:03:39,858 --> 00:03:44,362
That's the definition worth holding onto: the pattern is the design, not a sentence

66
00:03:44,362 --> 00:03:45,936
you add to sound responsible.

67
00:03:45,936 --> 00:03:47,293
The exercise is specific.

68
00:03:47,293 --> 00:03:51,472
Wherever your PRD mentions a human check, name the pattern precisely, and ask

69
00:03:51,472 --> 00:03:54,728
whether it matches how bad a wrong answer would actually be.

70
00:03:54,728 --> 00:03:59,286
A mismatch in either direction is worth fixing before launch, not after the reviewer

71
00:03:59,286 --> 00:04:01,511
burns out or the wrong one slips through.

72
00:04:02,061 --> 00:04:03,713
One thing before you move on.

73
00:04:03,713 --> 00:04:08,440
Name the pattern precisely, using this lecture's five options, not the vague phrase

74
00:04:08,440 --> 00:04:09,750
your PRD currently has.

75
00:04:09,750 --> 00:04:12,256
Then check it honestly against blast radius.

76
00:04:12,256 --> 00:04:17,040
If a wrong output is expensive or irreversible and your pattern is anything short of

77
00:04:17,040 --> 00:04:21,767
pre-approval, that mismatch is worth raising now, while it's a sentence to edit and

78
00:04:21,767 --> 00:04:23,305
not an incident to explain.

79
00:04:23,305 --> 00:04:27,633
Next lecture puts everything from this section, the dataset, the rubric, the

80
00:04:27,633 --> 00:04:32,189
threshold, the taxonomy, and this human-in-the-loop pattern, into the actual PRD

81
00:04:32,189 --> 00:04:34,695
template, walked through section by section.
